#: ../folio-assistant-sci/processes/content/lean-docs-publish.bpmn:91
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "`bun run cat state:mount` (or `branch-store.ts mount --id <id>`) at the start of the site build, as for `beans/`: puts the branch tip on disk at the declared path. Reads the RENDER; writes nothing to the branch. A site build of a folio whose Lean did not change starts here."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-sci/processes/content/lean-docs-publish.bpmn:105
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "A site step of the folio's declared build (`site.build.siteSteps`). Reads the mounted RENDER, writes the PUBLISHED pages at `<site>/<route>/`, the route the declaration's `coverage.visualiser` names: the KG viewer and its `data/` shards are copied as they are, and the site build's own rail pass (`rail-standalone-pages.ts --foreign-site`, which runs after every site step) puts the harness rail on the viewer page, so the rail's assets are the site's and nothing extra lands at its root. Fallback, when the store holds doc-gen4's static HTML instead: Tool `lean-render-publish` (`adapters/paper/lean/lean-render.ts --docs <mount path> --site <site> --route <route> --no-rail`) stages it without Lake's bookkeeping, marks its iframe navbar and `find/` redirect rail-declined and adds the layout shim. The document site's ∀ panel links each declaration to the viewer opened at it, `<route>/#/m/<Module>/<name>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-sci/processes/content/lean-docs-publish.bpmn:82
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Answered by `branch-store.ts push`: `pushed` (or `current`) continues; a `conflict` or a failed push stops here, and the previous render on the branch stays what a site build mounts."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-sci/processes/content/lean-docs-publish.bpmn:96
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Answered by `state:mount`'s row for the store: `mounted` continues; a `miss` (no such branch) or any failure stops here. An empty mount is not a pass: a site published from it would drop its Lean pages without saying so."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-sci/processes/content/lean-docs-publish.bpmn:65
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Answered by Task_Build's exit code: 0 and a render directory, or a refusal (2: a guard; 3: no warm cache) or a failed build (1). Only 0 continues."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-sci/processes/content/lean-docs-publish.bpmn:113
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Answered by Task_Publish's own closing assertion: a staged page carrying neither the harness rail nor the `folio-navbar: none` opt-out fails it (exit 1)."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-sci/processes/content/lean-docs-publish.bpmn:14
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Build a folio's Lean render — the doc-gen4 HTML of the Lake packages the folio holds — keep it on its own branch store, and publish it inside the folio's site with the harness navbar and rail. The visualiser is the Lean KNOWLEDGE-GRAPH viewer (owner, 2026-11-19: \"use thin html and dynamic loading\"): `lean-kg-publish` turns doc-gen4's per-module declaration data into one viewer page and JSON shards the page loads on demand. doc-gen4's static HTML is a non-default fallback only. Three places, and each step says which it reads and writes. The SOURCE is the Lake packages in the folio's own tree (`folio/<paper>/lean/`), never moved. The RENDER lives on a branch store the folio declares (a directory of graph typology `lean` with `source: { kind: \"branch\", keyedBy: \"tip\" }`, on a branch under `cat/folio-assistant-sci/`), mounted at the declared path by `state:mount` exactly as `beans/` is; it is never committed to the folio's main. The PUBLISHED pages are the site's route named by that declaration's `coverage.visualiser` (`_site/lean/index.html` → `lean/`). The build is a Lake run over the folio's warm olean cache: expensive, and able to damage the cache (a Lake build over an untraced restore evicts it), so it sits with the Lean toolchain and refuses rather than risks. Its output is pushed to the branch store (`branch-store.ts push --id <id>`), so a site build needs no Lean toolchain at all: it mounts the store and rails the render, a file pass that cannot touch the cache, after the site is built and before the chrome check and deploy. A site rebuilt without a Lean change starts at the mount. Only End_Ready publishes: a refused guard, a failed build, a store that did not take the push, a mount that failed and an unrailed page are each reported as such, never as a complete site without Lean docs."
msgstr ""

